<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phaistos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2000–1400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Phaestos, Festos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phaistos is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> archaeological site in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, with the remains of a Minoan palace and the town around it, one of the largest cities of Minoan Crete. First settled about 3600 BCE, it had a palace from about 2000 BCE, which was destroyed and rebuilt three times before its final destruction in the 15th century BCE. Federico Halbherr and Luigi Pernier excavated it from 1900, and in 1908 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos-disc">Phaistos Disc</a> was found in a basement room of the palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phaistos.jpg" alt="Phaistos" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerzy Strzelecki · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Festos1(js).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ancient city lay about 5.6 km east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and 62 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, at modern Faistos. The form Pa-i-to appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> texts and on 62 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. Phaistos was one of the three largest cities of Minoan Crete, with Knossos and Malia, and a road system linked it to the other cities. It was also connected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Hagia Triada</a>, which has a smaller palace that may have been a residence of the rulers of Phaistos, and to Gortyn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was first inhabited around 3600 BCE, slightly later than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. The first palace dates to the Middle Minoan IB period, around 2000 BCE, and it was destroyed and rebuilt three times over about three centuries. Each rebuilding expanded on the last, on several levels joined by halls and stairs, and the palace was about two thirds the size of Knossos. It held religious and political areas, storage, housing and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. It was destroyed around 1400 BCE and never rebuilt, perhaps by a large earthquake. Grape pips found in storage vessels indicate wine production.</p><p>The site was reoccupied in the Geometric Age, in the 8th century BCE. Phaistos then struck its own currency, the stater, and allied with other Cretan cities and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>. Around the end of the 3rd century BCE the Gortynians destroyed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Federico Halbherr and his student Luigi Pernier excavated the site from 1900 to 1904. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos-disc">Phaistos Disc</a> was found in 1908 in a basement room on the north side of the palace, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> tablet. Italian teams under Doro Levi, Vincenzo La Rosa and, from 2007, Fausto Longo have continued work, and 61 Linear A inscribed items have been found. In 2025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the site a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phaistos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1900 BCE, early in the Middle Minoan period, the first palaces were built at Knossos, <b>Phaistos</b> and Malia: large complexes around a central court where an elite stored produce, organised workshops and kept written records.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1950 BCE its first palace was built around a paved central court, and <b>Phaistos</b> became the leading power of southern Crete.</blockquote><a href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Palace State of Phaistos</a></li></ul></section>
